//! THE NODE MODEL'S OWN DECLARATION, AS ROWS. A node variant's
//! payloads — each name, spelling and SHAPE, in declaration order —
//! read off the enum by `@derive(Grammar)` and consumed by the
//! grammar's node binding. Order IS the contract: a build's arguments
//! are filled in payload order, so two same-typed payloads are told
//! apart by nothing else.
//!
//! A PAYLOAD IS A FIELD — the same shape the crossing hands an
//! annotation, never a second description carrying only the spelling.
//! The poorer copy is WHY a type's spelling got parsed as text: "is
//! this a list", "may it be absent", "does a token fill it" are read
//! off `kind`, and a row that carried only `ty` could answer them no
//! other way.
use @std.meta.{Field}

/// What a node build needs to know about the variant it names: its
/// payloads as FIELDS, in declaration order.
export type PayloadRow = { owner: string, variant: string, payloads: List<Field> }

/// The row for `owner.variant`, or nothing when the model declares
/// no such variant.
export fn row_for(rows: List<PayloadRow>, owner: string, variant: string) -> PayloadRow? {
    rows.find(it.owner == owner && it.variant == variant)
}
